        "howItWorks": "## groupby() â transform, aggregate, and filter\nGroupby operations often become bottlenecks when developers rely on slow custom lambdas instead of pandas' optimized, vectorized internal grouping engines.\nThink ofgroupby().apply()as sending each group off to a separate, independent calculation where a custom Python function runs.transform()is like a smart aggregator that, after calculating a group summary (e.g., sum), knows how to perfectly expand that single summary value back to match every row in its original group, efficiently without re-merging.\n`groupby().apply()``transform()`\n\n## The Setup\nYou are writing an analytics reporting routine that processes a transaction list grouped by user. You want to normalize each user's single transaction amount by dividing it by that specific user's total aggregate spend.\n\n\n## What Does This Print?\n\n```\nimport pandas as pd\nimport numpy as np\nimport time\n\n# Generate simulated transactional data\nnp.random.seed(42)\ndf = pd.DataFrame({\n    'user_id': np.random.randint(1, 1000, size=100000),\n    'amount': np.random.uniform(5, 500, size=100000)\n})\n\nstart = time.perf_counter()\n# Slow: calling apply with custom lambda that aggregates and divides\nresult_apply = df.groupby('user_id').apply(\n    lambda x: x['amount'] / x['amount'].sum(), \n    include_groups=False\n)\nprint(f\"Apply elapsed: {time.perf_counter() - start:.4f} seconds\")\n```\n`import pandas as pd\nimport numpy as np\nimport time\n\n# Generate simulated transactional data\nnp.random.seed(42)\ndf = pd.DataFrame({\n    'user_id': np.random.randint(1, 1000, size=100000),\n    'amount': np.random.uniform(5, 500, size=100000)\n})\n\nstart = time.perf_counter()\n# Slow: calling apply with custom lambda that aggregates and divides\nresult_apply = df.groupby('user_id').apply(\n    lambda x: x['amount'] / x['amount'].sum(), \n    include_groups=False\n)\nprint(f\"Apply elapsed: {time.perf_counter() - start:.4f} seconds\")`\n\n## The Output\nThe code takes about 1.5 to 2.5 seconds to compute..apply()here forc",
